#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:30
msgid "Docker Engine and Compose, ready to use over SSH."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:31
msgid "Log in over SSH to use Docker and Compose. Ports you publish with Docker bypass the firewall inside the server. Open or close them in the Firewall tab of your server, and bind to 127.0.0.1 the ports that only the server itself should use."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:32
msgid "n8n workflow automation with Caddy and PostgreSQL. Optional domain with HTTPS."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:33
msgid "Open your server address and sign in with the email and password shown once. The login and the encryption key are also stored on the server in /root/.beltoft-app/credentials.json. Keep the encryption key safe: without it, the logins you save in n8n cannot be restored on another server. Change the password in n8n's settings. Add a domain for HTTPS before you connect real accounts; without a domain the address is not encrypted. Outgoing mail on port 25 is blocked on all servers; use an SMTP service on port 587 or 465. Times in n8n use UTC until you change the timezone in settings. Set up a backup plan before you build workflows. You keep n8n up to date yourself, and you use it under n8n's Sustainable Use License (your own business use, not resale): https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/LICENSE.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:34
msgid "Nextcloud Server 35 (community) with PostgreSQL, Redis and Caddy. Optional domain with HTTPS."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:35
msgid "Open your server address and sign in as admin with the password shown once. The login is also stored on the server in /root/.beltoft-app/credentials.json, and your email address is set on the admin account. Add a domain for HTTPS before you connect the desktop and phone apps; without a domain the address is not encrypted and the apps need HTTPS. Calendar and contacts work over CalDAV and CardDAV at your server address. Your files are stored on the server disk, so a plan with more disk, or an extra disk, gives you more space. Outgoing mail on port 25 is blocked on all servers; set an SMTP service on port 587 or 465 in Nextcloud's Basic settings. Backups are your job: turn on maintenance mode with docker compose exec -u www-data app php occ maintenance:mode --on from /opt/nextcloud, back up the database with docker compose exec postgres pg_dump, copy the data volume (docker volume nextcloud_nextcloud-data), then turn maintenance mode off. Updates are manual and one major version at a time: pull the image of the next major version only, because Nextcloud cannot skip a major version. If the server address or domain changes, list the allowed ones with docker compose exec -u www-data app php occ config:system:get trusted_domains from /opt/nextcloud and add the new one under the next free number with occ config:system:set trusted_domains NUMBER --value=NEW-ADDRESS. Nextcloud Server is licensed under the AGPL-3.0."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:36
msgid "Odoo Community 20 with Caddy and PostgreSQL. Optional domain with HTTPS."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:37
msgid "Open your server address and sign in with the email and password shown once. The login and the master password are also stored on the server in /root/.beltoft-app/credentials.json. The master password protects database management; Odoo's database manager is turned off for safety, so it is only needed if you turn it on again in /opt/odoo/config/odoo.conf. Add a domain for HTTPS before real use; without a domain the address is not encrypted. Outgoing mail on port 25 is blocked on all servers; use an SMTP service on port 587 or 465 in Odoo's outgoing mail server settings. Backups are your job: because the database manager is off, back up the database with docker compose exec postgres pg_dump from /opt/odoo, and copy the filestore volume (docker volume odoo_odoo-data). Updates are manual: in /opt/odoo/compose.yaml replace the odoo image digest with a newer 20.0 one, run docker compose pull, then docker compose run --rm odoo odoo -d odoo -u all --stop-after-init, then docker compose up -d. This is Odoo Community, licensed under the LGPL-3.0; Enterprise apps need an Odoo subscription. The smallest plan runs Odoo for one or two users; for more users or several apps, choose 2 cores and 4 GB of memory or more."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:38
msgid "Plesk Obsidian web hosting control panel, installed and licensed. Choose a Plesk edition when you order."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:39
msgid "Open https://your-server-address:8443 and sign in as admin with the password shown once. The login is also stored on the server in /root/.beltoft-app/credentials.json. Your browser warns about the certificate at first, because Plesk starts with a certificate of its own; secure the panel with a Let's Encrypt certificate for a host name in Tools and Settings. The Plesk licence matches the edition you chose and is tied to the address of this server. It ends when you cancel the Plesk subscription or delete the server, and you can switch edition on this page. Plesk keeps itself up to date. The firewall opens ports 22, 80, 443 and 8443; open more in the firewall tab if you need them. Outgoing mail on port 25 is blocked on all servers; use an SMTP service on port 587 or 465."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:40
msgid "WordPress with Caddy, PHP 8.3 and MariaDB. Optional domain with HTTPS."
msgstr ""

#: src/Apps/AppTexts.php:41
msgid "Open /wp-admin/ at your server address and sign in with the admin login shown once. The login is also stored on the server in /root/.beltoft-app/credentials.json. Add a domain for HTTPS. Configure SMTP on port 587 before relying on site email. Set up a backup plan before making changes. You keep WordPress, plugins and themes up to date yourself."
msgstr ""

